    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d396">

                <pb facs="0645" n="617" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v03/pg617"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">320/9–2853: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The United States Representative at the United
                        Nations</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Lodge</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">New York</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1953-09-28T13:00:00-05:00">September 28,
                            1953—1 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    Delga 39. Re United Nations staff assessments,
                        Deptel 115.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d396-f1"
                    /> I have asked Ambassador Wadsworth to handle this question for me. It is his <pb
                        facs="0646" n="618" xml:id="pg618"/> view that on administrative and legal
                    grounds it would be preferable to adopt tax exemption legislation. Such
                    legislation would be difficult to obtain at present, in Ambassador Wadsworth’s view. Accordingly, he
                    recommends Department request tax credit legislation for United States nationals
                    in international organizations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lodge</hi>
                        </signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
